  module: "Module 1: How a Chip Is Physically Made",
  title: "Annealing and Thermal Processing — How Heat Repairs the Wafer and Activates Dopants",
  date: "2026-08-17",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 006: Deposition adds ultra-thin films, and increasingly three-dimensional chip structures increase the value of precise deposition methods such as ALD.

Lesson 007: Etch removes selected material after lithography defines the pattern, and rising 3D complexity increases the value of high-precision plasma etch.

Lesson 008: Process control uses inspection, metrology and analytics to catch defects early, protect yield and stop fabs from adding value to wafers that are already bad.

Lesson 009: Yield measures how much manufactured output becomes usable product, and small yield differences can create large cost and gross-margin differences because the same wafer cost is spread across more or fewer good dies.

Lesson 010: Doping changes silicon's electrical behavior, and ion implantation places dopant atoms at controlled dose, depth and angle before thermal processing repairs the crystal and activates those dopants.

The big idea

Yesterday we intentionally fired energetic ions into a silicon wafer.

That solved one problem: it placed dopant atoms where the transistor needs them.

But it created another problem.

The impact of those ions damages the silicon crystal, and many implanted dopant atoms are not yet electrically active.

The fab therefore heats the wafer.

This is annealing.

Annealing uses carefully controlled heat to repair crystal damage, activate dopants and modify material properties without allowing the wafer to become too hot for too long.

The simplest mental model is:

Ion implantation creates the right dopant distribution but damages the crystal → annealing repairs the lattice and activates the dopants → the transistor can behave as designed.

The difficult part is that heat is both useful and dangerous.

1. Why the silicon crystal needs repair

Silicon atoms in a wafer are arranged in a highly ordered crystal lattice.

Ion implantation shoots dopant ions into that lattice at high speed.

The ions collide with silicon atoms and knock some of them away from their ideal positions.

Think of a perfectly organized grid of billiard balls.

Now fire additional balls into the grid.

You can place the new balls inside, but the collisions disrupt the original arrangement.

That is roughly what implantation does at atomic scale.

If the fab did nothing afterward, the damaged lattice would hurt the transistor's electrical behavior.

Annealing gives atoms enough thermal energy to move toward lower-energy, more orderly positions.

The crystal can partially repair itself.

2. Annealing also activates the dopants

Repairing damage is only half the job.

A dopant atom such as boron or phosphorus does not automatically help conduct electrical charge simply because it is physically inside the wafer.

It usually needs to sit in the right kind of location within the silicon lattice.

When that happens, the dopant becomes electrically active.

So annealing performs two linked jobs:

- repair implantation damage;
- activate implanted dopants.

This is why yesterday's implant step and today's thermal step should be thought of as a pair.

Implant decides where the dopants go.

Anneal helps make those dopants electrically useful.

3. The dangerous side of heat: diffusion

If heating were only beneficial, the fab could simply cook the wafer for a long time.

It cannot.

Heat also causes atoms to move.

That movement is called diffusion.

Suppose a fab carefully implants dopants into a very shallow region only a few nanometers from the surface.

If the wafer stays hot for too long, those dopants can spread away from their intended positions.

The transistor feature becomes less sharply defined.
